Silent fall-through instead of an explicit rejection. Both the download-scope branch (line 37) and the blacklist branch here abort authentication and simply continue the chain. Because `SecurityConfig` only registers an `accessDeniedHandler` and no `AuthenticationEntryPoint`, unauthenticated requests fall to Spring Security's default `Http403ForbiddenEntryPoint`, i.e. the client receives an **empty 403** — not the 401 JSON this filter produces everywhere else (`writeUnauthorized`). The front end distinguishes 401 (re-login) from 403 (no permission), so a logged-out/revoked token is reported as a permission error instead of a session timeout. This is exactly the "空 403" symptom the catch-block comment below says was fixed; it is still reachable here. Suggest rejecting explicitly:

```java
if (jwtTokenProvider.isBlacklisted(jwtTokenProvider.getJtiFromToken(token))) {
    writeUnauthorized(response);
    return;
}
```
and doing the same for the download-scope mismatch branch.

